is the 10,000 hour rule actually true  we'll find out in this video so  originally asked a question for one of  my YouTube viewers Sally love you guys  how many hours per day would you  recommend to work at a particular skill  in total per week to become outstanding  at something so while ago in this famous  book outliers Malcolm Gladwell came up  with a 10,000 hour rule his theory was  that if you spend 10,000 hours working  on something then you would get to  master class level he use examples of  the Beatles who toured Germany in the  early sixties and built up their 10,000  hours of practice and Bill Gates who got  his first computer as a young teenager  and spent over 10,000 hours programming  on it but do you actually need to spend  10,000 hours to become world-class at a  particular skill so here are my three  thoughts on it number one your passion  matters you can spend hours and hours  and hours and hours working at something  and if you're not passionate about it  you're not going to become master class  level just think about when you're in  school there are some subjects that you  love and some subjects that you hated  and no matter how much time you spent on  the subject you hated your growth would  grow so slowly and painfully but the  subjects that you loved and will  generally interested in you would learn  so much faster and that's a problem with  most people today they're working the  jobs that they hate they graduated with  whatever degree entered the workforce  and they spend 10 20 30 40 50 years  working at a company that they hate you  can spend 10 years at a company and  never get that big promotion never get  to be the CEO or anywhere close to it  even though you spent the ten thousand  hours plus 20 thousand hours 30 thousand  hours you're never getting to that level  where you want to be because you're not  passionate enough about it if you are  genuinely interested in the subject  you're learning you're going to pick it  up much more quickly than if you're  forced to do something that you hate  number two is not all hours are the same  the fastest way to pick something up the  fastest way to learn a new skill is to  model success get a coach get a trainer  get a teacher get somebody  show you the ropes the slowest way to  figure something out is to try to  understand that yourself and make all  the mistakes that people have already  made so 10,000 hours trying to struggle  to figure it all out for yourself may be  equivalent to 500 hours of being taught  by professional and how to do it I look  back on my youtube channel as an example  in my first videos I didn't really know  what I was doing I was experimenting I  was trying to do everything myself  and my first videos are really really  really bad and slowly I got better but  it was a slow crawl forward go look back  on my channel and you'll see years and  years of just slow development and then  when I met Jamie who's my media coach  she trained me on how to get better and  within a couple months I was already  seen significant growth on my channel  now I still worked hard I'm still  putting in the effort I'm still applying  everything that she's teaching me and as  a result the growth in my development  the growth in my subscribers a growth in  my views on each video has grown  tremendously  so not all hours are created equal if  you model success and learn from the  best you'll achieve results a lot faster  than someone who tries to do everything  themselves and my third point is why do  you really care and I ask this because  if you're asking how many hours it takes  to get really good at something how many  hours you need to dedicate in your week  to to get good at this particular skill  then it kind of tells you that you're  not really that interested in learning  the skill if you really want to get  better at something then you should feel  like you're getting lost in it like you  could wake up in the morning and start  working on it and then look up and it's  already 6 o'clock at night and you're  not keeping track of the hours to say  only a hundred more hours until I get  really good at this  it's like someone who starts a business  just trying to make a lot of money you  find that they never do they struggle  they struggle they struggle they tell  themself if I keep this struggle up  adventure I'm going to make my millions  of dollars but they never get there  because they never loved it in the first  place you should want to get better at  that skill it should drive you it should  feel you should make you happy and  excited to learn and get better and if  you have a number in your head that  that's your end goal like when I get to  number of hours I'm going to be really  good and then I'm going to achieve  success that's the wrong way to look at  it instead you should find a skill or a  goal that you really want to get better  at something where you stop tracking the  hours something that you can really have  a positive impact on the world with and  if that's your intention and you work  hard at it you will achieve masterclass  low believe I love to know what your  thoughts are leave a comment below let  me know how cause a little bit of a  controversy or not here and if you like  this video you want to see more don't  forget to subscribe to the channel thank  you so much for watching I'll see you  soon self-improvement books are like  Fitness books they never work on their  own what you need is a trainer that's  Evan gab the newsletter  you
 

